  "answer": "To reduce the risk of reward misspecification—where humans might inadvertently train an AI to favor deceptive or unsafe strategies because end-to-end, outcome-based optimization hides the model's internal reasoning—process supervision has been proposed (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/FFz6H35Gy6BArHxkc/task-decomposition-for-scalable-oversight-agisf-distillation). The mechanism involves incorporating step-level human feedback during the training phase; human raters evaluate and label each intermediate reasoning step (e.g., on a math scratch-pad), and these dense, step-level labels are used as rewards or supervised targets during fine-tuning to keep the model's reasoning transparent and aligned (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/FFz6H35Gy6BArHxkc/task-decomposition-for-scalable-oversight-agisf-distillation)."
